title: Splunk SPL, hands-on
guide: howto/splunk-spl.html
promise: Learn Splunk's search language from zero on 24 CloudTrail events, and learn to spot the search that returns nothing because it cannot work.

== Who it is for and what you need
section: How to use this guide
It is for beginners new to Splunk and query languages who can use a terminal. Allow about two hours.

You need Docker with about 3 GB of memory and 3 GB of disk free, but no Splunk or cloud account.

== How the guide was checked
section: How to use this guide
A script ran every command and search in order on Splunk Enterprise 10.4.3 in Docker, checking each result against the guide.

Not run: Splunk Cloud Platform, Splunk Enterprise Security, the Splunk Add-on for AWS and the Splunk Free licence.

== How to follow the guide
section: How to use this guide
Read it in order. Paste BASH blocks into your terminal. Run SPL blocks with ./spl from step 1, or in Splunk Web, the browser interface. Unlabelled blocks show what you should see. "Show the answer" opens each exercise answer.

== Set up your environment
section: 1. Start Splunk Enterprise in Docker
These commands start Splunk and accept the licence and Splunk General Terms for you, so read them first. On an Apple silicon Mac, turn on Docker Desktop's Rosetta setting. The last line waits until Docker reports the container healthy.
```bash
mkdir -p ~/splunk-lab && cd ~/splunk-lab
openssl rand -hex 12 > .splunk-password
docker run -d --name splunk-lab --platform linux/amd64 \
  -p 8000:8000 -p 8089:8089 \
  -e SPLUNK_START_ARGS=--accept-license \
  -e SPLUNK_GENERAL_TERMS=--accept-sgt-current-at-splunk-com \
  -e SPLUNK_PASSWORD="$(cat .splunk-password)" \
  splunk/splunk:10.4.3
until [ "$(docker inspect -f '{{.State.Health.Status}}' splunk-lab)" = healthy ]; do sleep 10; done
```

== What SPL is and where you meet it
section: The language of Splunk searches
Splunk stores logs and lets you search them with its Search Processing Language (SPL), in Splunk Enterprise or Splunk Cloud Platform.

Security analysts type SPL to investigate. Detection engineers save SPL searches that run on a schedule and raise alerts.

== A search is a pipeline
section: A search is a pipeline
The base search, before the first pipe character (|), chooses events. Each | hands the results to the next command.

Every prefix of a search is a search you can run, so to debug one, delete commands from the end.
```spl
index=cloudtrail readOnly=false
| stats count BY userIdentity.userName
```

== The lab: 24 CloudTrail events and a leaked key
The lab is 24 events from CloudTrail, the record of calls made to an Amazon Web Services (AWS) account.

A continuous integration user, build-bot, has leaked its access key, the credential a program signs its AWS requests with.

== What someone does with build-bot's key
From 203.0.113.50 someone probes the account and is mostly refused, creates the user backup-svc with administrator rights and a key, and uses it to stop CloudTrail logging.

Around that runs ordinary activity, including a sign-in without multi-factor authentication (MFA).

== include: _cloudtrail-lab.deck

== A load can report success and still be wrong
section: 2. Load the lab data, then check what arrived
A source type tells Splunk how to split text into events and find each event's time. The oneshot load can print Oneshot ... added even when every event was dropped. This check wants 24 events, each with its own time.
```spl
index=cloudtrail
| eval own_time=if(_time == strptime(eventTime, "%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S%Z"), "yes", "no")
| stats count BY sourcetype own_time
```

== Logins without MFA (D4): require success too
section: 4. Nested fields, and where versus the base search: logins without MFA (D4)
The first draft checked only the multi-factor authentication (MFA) flag and also returned someone failing to sign in as alice, since a failed sign-in carries MFAUsed No too. With Success required, only bob's sign-in comes back.
```spl
index=cloudtrail eventName=ConsoleLogin additionalEventData.MFAUsed=No responseElements.ConsoleLogin=Success
| table _time userIdentity.userName sourceIPAddress
```

== Before you trust an empty result, run a control
section: 5. Count, then threshold: the burst that never fires (D2)
Matching only errorCode=AccessDenied, the D2 draft printed nothing. This control shows why: Elastic Compute Cloud (EC2) reported three of the attacker's refusals as Client.UnauthorizedOperation. The fillnull command keeps the 14 events with no errorCode, which stats would silently drop.
```spl
index=cloudtrail
| fillnull value="(none)" errorCode
| stats count BY errorCode
```

== Turn D2 into a scheduled alert
section: 7. Turn D2 into a scheduled alert
An alert is a search, schedule, time window and condition. The search lists four codes starting AccessDenied or ending UnauthorizedOperation. The window ends ten minutes ago to allow for CloudTrail's delivery delay.
```ini
search = index=cloudtrail errorCode IN (AccessDenied, AccessDeniedException, Client.UnauthorizedOperation, UnauthorizedOperation) \
| stats count BY sourceIPAddress \
| where count >= 5
enableSched = 1
cron_schedule = */10 * * * *
dispatch.earliest_time = -20m@m
dispatch.latest_time = -10m@m
counttype = number of events
relation = greater than
quantity = 0
```

== Test the alert both ways
section: 8. Test the alert both ways
Run the alert over a quiet window and the attack window, then count firings. The leading | rest reads Splunk's own REST API from inside a search. Two runs, one firing: the attack window fired, the quiet one did not.
```spl
| rest /servicesNS/-/-/alerts/fired_alerts splunk_server=local
| table title triggered_alert_count
```

== Splunk's rule for D1 assumes its field names
section: 9. Splunk's public rules: the security_content format
Its MITRE ATT&CK ID is the v19 one, but the references field still names T1562.008, which v19 revoked. Pointed at the lab's index, not the cloudtrail macro (sourcetype=aws:cloudtrail), it misses the StopLogging event: raw CloudTrail has no errorCode on success.
```yaml
    `cloudtrail` eventName = StopLogging eventSource = cloudtrail.amazonaws.com userAgent!=console.amazonaws.com errorCode = success
      | rename user_name as user
# (trimmed: the search goes on to count by signature, dest, user, src and vendor fields)
how_to_implement: You must install Splunk AWS Add on and enable Cloudtrail logs in your AWS Environment.
references:
    - https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1562/008/
```
credit: From Splunk's splunk/security_content repository (github.com/splunk/security_content), trimmed.

== Exercise: this search returns nothing
section: Hands-on exercises
Find both faults, and say how you would prove each one rather than guess.
```spl
eventName=StopLogging
| where userIdentity.userName="backup-svc"
| table _time eventName userIdentity.userName
```

== Answer: two faults, found by deleting from the end
section: Hands-on exercises
With no index, Splunk searched only the default indexes. And the where command reads the unquoted dotted name as two empty strings joined. Fixing either alone still returns nothing, so prove each by deleting commands from the end.
```spl
index=cloudtrail eventName=StopLogging
| where 'userIdentity.userName'="backup-svc"
| table _time eventName userIdentity.userName
```

== Exercise: write D3 with stats instead of transaction
section: Hands-on exercises
In the guide's step 6, the transaction command groups each new user's events from CreateUser to CreateAccessKey within 10 minutes. Write D3 with stats instead, so svc-reporting, keyed hours after creation, stays out. Then say what each condition protects against.
```spl
index=cloudtrail eventName IN (CreateUser, CreateAccessKey)
| transaction requestParameters.userName startswith=eval(eventName="CreateUser") endswith=eval(eventName="CreateAccessKey") maxspan=10m
| table _time requestParameters.userName userIdentity.userName sourceIPAddress duration eventcount
```

== Answer: three conditions in one where command
section: Hands-on exercises
The dc function counts distinct values, so steps=2 needs both events. The earliest function gives the first event's name, so the user must be created first. range(_time) is last minus first, in seconds, and gap<=600 is the ten-minute window.
```spl
index=cloudtrail eventName IN (CreateUser, CreateAccessKey)
| stats earliest(eventName) AS first_step dc(eventName) AS steps range(_time) AS gap BY requestParameters.userName
| where steps=2 AND first_step="CreateUser" AND gap<=600
```

== Common mistakes
section: Common mistakes
- A missing index=, a mis-cased field name and Splunk Web's default Last 24 hours all return nothing, like a quiet account.
- The where command is case-sensitive and needs single quotes around dotted names.
- stats drops events lacking the BY field.
